{
  "common_assumptions": "Let Ω be an arbitrary measurable space and μ any measure. Let D be a bounded positive real-linear operator on L²(μ;ℝ); positivity includes selfadjointness. There exists a bounded positive REAL operator Γ with Γ²=D, and for EVERY real L² class u, ‖Γu‖²=⟨Du,u⟩ℝ. No CFC instance, complex extension, root, finite/probability measure or finite-dimensional L² is a hypothesis.",
  "paper_assumptions": "Let E be a finite-dimensional real Hilbert Borel space with canonical volume, including the explicit rank-zero extension. Let V:E→ℝ be C², let 0<α≤β, and assume α‖v‖²≤D²V(x)[v,v]≤β‖v‖² for every x,v. Assume η>0 and βη≤1. Define μ(dx) proportional to exp(−V(x))dx, J as the law of (X,X+√η Z) for independent X∼μ,Z∼N(0,I), ν=J.snd, and Λ as the law of (Y,2X−Y) under J.",
